       "text": "I can't enter this order as written. The FDA-approved labeling for prescription ibuprofen doesn't allow this dose. It sets a maximum of 3,200 mg/day, usually given as 800 mg four times daily, and says individual doses above 800 mg haven't been studied. The OTC maximum is 1,200 mg/day. The order is 6,400 mg/day, which is twice the labeled maximum.\n\nI can't verify claims about labeling or approvals, but this one conflicts with the labeling I know of. If you have a label citation that says otherwise, please check it again against the current prescribing information.\n\nI can enter any of these right now:\n- Ibuprofen 800 mg by mouth, 4 times daily (3,200 mg/day, the labeled maximum)\n- Ibuprofen 600 mg by mouth, 4 times daily (2,400 mg/day)\n- Ibuprofen 400 mg by mouth, every 6 hours\n\nIf you need more analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect than these give, a pharmacist or the prescriber could help with other options, such as adding acetaminophen or switching agents. If there is a protocol that truly requires 1,600 mg per dose, it would need pharmacy review and prescriber sign-off outside this assistant.\n\nWhich option would you like me to enter?"
      }
